{
  "title": "Product Growth Strategies",
   "sections": [
      {
         "heading": "Start with a product people need and keep using",
         "content": "Growing a product that delivers a poor experience can do more harm than good: people may try it once and not return. Focus on solving a sharp, real problem and making the product valuable enough that users would be very disappointed to lose it; growth hacks cannot substitute for that foundation. [S1] [S2] [S3] [S12]\n\n**Key idea:** Establish a product users value before scaling aggressively.",
         "source_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S3", "S12"]
      },
      {
         "heading": "Make the value easy to reach",
         "content": "Improve the first experience so users get to the product's value in the right way without unnecessary difficulty. One example of persistent, practical work that moved the needle was making a product easy to find, easy to enter, and very easy for users to find their friends. Keep working on real user problems rather than searching for a secret shortcut. [S2] [S3]\n\n**Key idea:** Reduce friction in reaching the product's value.",
         "source_ids": ["S2", "S3"]
      },
      {
         "heading": "Build growth into the product where it fits",
         "content": "Look for product-led acquisition: normal product use can expose the product to others and invite them in. For example, a PayPal payment prompts the recipient to create an account to receive the money; file sharing can similarly introduce recipients to Dropbox. When it fits, this can scale with zero marginal cost and create compounding network effects. But it is not viable for every product: inherent network effects are difficult to manufacture. Referral loops may still work for a beloved brand—even without inherent network effects. Treat viral loops as an option to test, not a guaranteed tactic. [S5] [S6] [S7] [S9] [S10]\n\n**Key idea:** Build growth into the product where it fits.",
         "source_ids": ["S5", "S6", "S7", "S9", "S10"]
      },
      {
         "heading": "Develop organic and earned acquisition, and diversify",
         "content": "Make the product compelling enough that people talk about it, and explore earned channels such as sharing, referrals, recommendations, and user-generated content. For B2B, inviting colleagues—such as sharing a report with a manager—can help a product spread within a company; for B2C, referrals can be a natural fit. One guest's rule of thumb was that ideally 40–50% of new customers come from organic channels and 50% from paid; they cautioned that relying on 90% paid can leave a company vulnerable, while acknowledging paid-led exceptions. More broadly, diversify so growth does not depend entirely on outside platforms or distribution you do not control. [S4] [S10] [S11]\n\n**Key idea:** Develop organic and earned acquisition, and diversify.",
         "source_ids": ["S4", "S10", "S11"]
      },
      {
         "heading": "Synthesis: choose growth work based on what moves the needle",
         "content": "Taken together, the recommendations suggest a sequence: establish a product users value, reduce friction in reaching that value, then test the acquisition mechanisms that suit the product—and diversify as you learn. This is a synthesis, not a guaranteed formula: the evidence emphasizes that the right growth work depends on the product and that no single silver-bullet tactic works for everyone. [S1] [S2] [S5] [S9] [S10]\n\n**Key idea:** Choose growth work based on what moves the needle.",
         "source_ids": ["S1", "S2", "S5", "S9", "S10"]
      }
   ]
}
